The logical kernel is separate from that numbered registry. Prov.v defines K, S, contraposition, modus ponens, generalization, universal instantiation, vacuous quantification, quantifier distribution, reflexivity, and Leibniz substitution as proof constructors. Replay2.v exposes corresponding checked steps, including SAx, SMp, SGen, SInst, SLeib, STaut, existential introduction and elimination, and conditional universal introduction. Syn.v defines conjunction Kf, disjunction Df, biconditional Qf, and existential quantification Xf as abbreviations. These are no numbered registry entries. The table therefore records the kernel route without assigning a registry index to it.

LOGIC-BOOLEAN-CHECK.rkt independently parses the exact source formulas for 15 propositional axiom labels and enumerates all 84 integer 0/1 assignments. LOGIC-BOOLEAN-CHECK.tsv records 0 false assignments for each label. The labels are ax-1, ax-2, ax-3, ax-luk1, ax-luk2, ax-luk3, ax-frege1, ax-frege2, ax-frege8, ax-frege28, ax-frege31, ax-frege41, ax-frege52a, ax-frege54a, and ax-frege58a. The parser handles source negation, implication, biconditional, conjunction, disjunction, and if-. This validates their propositional truth after the stated operator interpretation. It does not claim a JDG1 proof or judge acceptance.

In LOGIC-MATCHES.tsv the prefix notation N, I, A[x], K, D, Q, X[x], Eq, and Mem denotes negation, implication, universal quantification, conjunction, disjunction, biconditional, existential quantification, equality, and membership. K(a,b)=N(I(a,N(b))), D(a,b)=I(N(a),b), Q(a,b)=K(I(a,b),I(b,a)), and X[x](a)=N(A[x](N(a))). A source class operand in Mem or ClassEq still needs the explicit class interpretation. Merely having no class-abstraction tokens in a theorem does not make its class variables set variables.

The operator signatures count semantic operands and binders. They differ from the full list of source mandatory variables when a source definition carries fresh dummy variables or a justification hypothesis. For example, df-sb has 1 wff operand, 1 set binder, and 1 set term; fresh y and z occur in its source justification and are retained separately in the table. df-mo has 1 wff operand and 1 set binder; fresh y and z are source auxiliaries. df-ich has 1 wff operand and 2 set binders, with a fresh auxiliary variable a. The x in df-tru is bound, so its result is a closed formula with arity 0.

A fixed predicate-symbol arity in the judge counts free set-variable arguments. It does not count wff operands or binders. Consequently a signature such as 2wff for df-an is a connective-schema arity, not a fixed arity-2 set predicate. The faithful implementation is expansion into the kernel syntax. For a specific fully lowered instance f with k free set variables, a conservative predicate alias could have arity k and defining formula `forall v0 ... forall v(k-1) (P_f(v0,...,v(k-1)) <-> f)`. That instance-specific alias does not provide a generic operation taking two arbitrary formulas as inputs.

For ax-* labels, a conservative definition `P(args) <-> F` does not establish F. Missing axioms need the stated axiom or a derivation; missing inference rules need a rule or a derived-rule implementation. ax-mp and ax-gen have essential hypotheses and are rules. Their table entries retain those hypotheses. The same distinction applies to ax-prv1, whose premise is ph and whose conclusion is Prv ph.

Prv is primitive source syntax, declared without a definition. ax-prv1 is necessitation, ax-prv2 is provability distribution, and ax-prv3 is positive introspection. No fixed first-order registry definition supplies these schemata. Replacing Prv by the identity connective would change the source interpretation. A translation needs an explicit interpretation of provability or an extension with that modal syntax and its rules.

